00:04:44.222 --> 00:04:45.180
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:47.580
Well, so, can you tell
us a little bit more

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:50.810
about what exactly is DevOps
and what exactly is SRE?

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:51.790
How are they different?

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:53.580
How are they connected?

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.890
SETH VARGO: DevOps is
a cultural movement

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:58.860
that was started around
2009, officially.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:01.050
But there's a lot of
recorded incidents

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:04.710
of DevOps occurring all the
way back to 2003 and 2004.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:07.260
It really refers
to this condition

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:10.500
that happens in many
organizations, small and large,

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:14.280
that operators and developers
have this divide where

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:17.880
developers are responsible for
writing code and accelerating

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:18.750
the business.

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:20.970
And operators are responsible
for keeping that code

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:23.310
running and keeping
the business stable.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:24.690
Inevitably, what
would happen was

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:26.370
that developers would
write this code,

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:28.950
and they would throw it
over the metaphorical wall

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:31.350
to the operations team and
say, keep this running.

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:33.900
Keep my Java app, keep my Ruby
app, keep my Python app up

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:34.830
and running.

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:36.960
But operators would have
little or no understanding

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.880
of the language
it was written in,

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:40.530
the requirements
of the application,

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:42.407
or the business goals
of the application.

00:05:42.407 --> 00:05:44.490
So inevitably, they were
fighting an uphill battle

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:46.140
continuously, where
they were being

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:48.990
asked to monitor
and maintain systems

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:50.490
which they had no input in.

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:53.740
And they had no ability to push
back on the developers and say,

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:55.660
you know, hey, this
code isn't so great,

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:57.670
or this isn't going
to work at scale.

00:05:57.670 --> 00:05:59.430
And this challenge
ultimately led

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:01.620
to this idea of
DevOps, which is, what

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:03.430
if we eliminate that barrier?

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:06.930
What if we force or encourage
developers and operators

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:10.410
to work together in a way
such that the operators bring

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:13.170
their experience in
running systems at scale

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:14.700
and distributed systems.

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:16.260
And developers bring
their experience

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:19.680
of coding and
algorithms together so

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:22.510
that we can build better systems
and more reliable systems.

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:24.360
And that's really
the crux of DevOps.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:27.430
There's kind of some
sub-pillars that come with that.

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:29.970
So there's these kind
of six or seven things,

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:32.940
like reduce organizational
silos is a big one in DevOps.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:35.500
We want to just
reduce the bureaucracy

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:38.070
and these artificial walls
that exist between groups.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:40.215
Accepting failure as
normal is another big one.

00:06:40.215 --> 00:06:41.850
One of the big
challenges, and one

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:44.225
of the reasons there was such
a divide between developers

00:06:44.225 --> 00:06:47.580
and operators, is that if at
any point something went wrong,

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:49.020
it was all about
blaming someone.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:49.680
Well, who was at fault?

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:50.910
Who can we blame for this?

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:52.764
Whose budget do we
deduct this from?

00:06:52.764 --> 00:06:54.180
The third was that
we very quickly

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.610
realized that these huge,
waterfall-like developments

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:58.950
where we ship a million
lines of code at once

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:01.830
was not a great way to be
able to recover from failure.

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:02.992
So first we accept failure.

00:07:02.992 --> 00:07:04.950
But then one of the ways
to reduce that failure

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:06.460
is to just do
incremental changes.

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:09.300
So instead of shipping these
huge, brand new features,

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.340
let's just ship one
small thing at a time,

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:13.320
leveraging things like
tooling and automation

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.510
to make sure that we're not
spending all of our time

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:17.670
touching systems but
are instead automating

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:19.290
things and measurement.

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:20.872
Another challenge
with this, and part

00:07:20.872 --> 00:07:22.830
of the reason that the
wall can be broken down,

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:24.455
is that when we start
measuring things,

00:07:24.455 --> 00:07:27.600
we start talking quantitatively
instead of qualitatively.

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:29.490
It's much easier to
have a conversation.

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:32.204
So those are just a few
of the DevOps pillars.

00:07:32.204 --> 00:07:33.120
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:34.380
MARK MANDEL: So Liz, SRE--

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:35.370
what's that all about?

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:36.540
LIZ FONG-JONES: The
practice of SRE at Google

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:38.670
started in the early
2000s, in response

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:41.060
to many of the same pressures
that Seth is describing

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:45.330
of having systems not really be
resilient and not be scalable

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:47.850
when you're trying to build
and run some of the largest

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:49.650
software systems in existence.

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:52.140
So Google engineers
realized that they

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:55.710
needed to apply the techniques
of software development

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:58.320
to operations, that you
couldn't run operations

00:07:58.320 --> 00:07:59.279
as a separate function.

00:07:59.279 --> 00:08:00.778
But instead, that
you needed to have

00:08:00.778 --> 00:08:03.210
people with a combination of
operation skills and software

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:06.720
development skills working
together to run and design

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:07.670
these systems.

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:10.890
So SRE is a combination
of several things in that

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:14.640
it's both a job title that
a bunch of humans at Google

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:16.440
have-- such as myself--

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:19.814
and it's also a mindset
and a organization.

00:08:19.814 --> 00:08:21.480
And it's a set of
engineering approaches

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:24.100
that we use to run better
production systems.

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:28.590
We tend to focus a lot on
measuring service reliability

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:30.660
and identifying, what
engineering changes do

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:33.201
we need to make to systems in
order to make sure that they're

00:08:33.201 --> 00:08:34.620
more scalable and maintainable?

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:38.059
And this can mean everything
from doing measurement

00:08:38.059 --> 00:08:41.059
of our systems to make sure that
they're appropriately reliable,

00:08:41.059 --> 00:08:43.832
to developing automation,
to being intimately involved

00:08:43.832 --> 00:08:45.290
in the design and
figuring out kind

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:47.240
of how do we simplify systems.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:48.887
So it started at
Google originally

00:08:48.887 --> 00:08:50.720
but spread to many,
many different companies

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:51.980
beyond Google.

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:55.050
But in the end, it kind of
solves the same problems

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:57.860
that Seth identified of
having a wall between software

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:00.542
development and operations not
being a desirable thing at all.

00:09:00.542 --> 00:09:02.000
MARK MANDEL: It
sounds like there's

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:05.570
a lot of overlap
between SRE and DevOps.

00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:06.650
Should I be doing SRE?

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:07.700
Should I be doing DevOps?

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:08.491
Which one's better?

00:09:08.491 --> 00:09:09.151
Which one wins?

00:09:09.151 --> 00:09:10.650
LIZ FONG-JONES:
It's all contextual.

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:13.160
It depends on what's
most likely to work

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:14.900
in your own organization.

00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:17.870
It definitely is the case that
because of the fact that there

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:19.850
are so many different
commonalities between SRE

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:22.910
and DevOps, you can
almost think of DevOps

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:25.310
as being an abstract
class or interface,

00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:28.760
and the SRE class being
a concrete implementation

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:30.870
of the DevOps principles.

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:33.540
So, for instance, Seth
talked about the idea

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:36.710
of breaking down silos and
trying to use common tooling.

00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:40.010
And that's something that
definitely SREs feel and SREs

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:43.610
do, in that we have
the same set of tooling

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:45.590
that we use on product
development and software

00:09:45.590 --> 00:09:48.890
engineering teams and SRE teams.

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:50.510
We do measurement.

00:09:50.510 --> 00:09:53.540
We measure the level
of service reliability.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:55.730
We have service-level
objectives, which we'll maybe

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:57.470
talk about in a little bit.

00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:01.340
We have the notion of using
automation and tooling to solve

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:02.467
problems whenever possible.

00:10:02.467 --> 00:10:04.466
That's kind of the building
software part of SRE

00:10:04.466 --> 00:10:06.360
that I talked about earlier.

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:08.870
So in all these
various ways, SRE

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:11.480
is a very concrete and
specific and prescriptive

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:14.930
way of accomplishing the
goals that DevOps also

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:15.950
sets out to solve.

00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:18.159
MELANIE WARRICK: Would you
ever call yourself DevOps?

00:10:18.159 --> 00:10:20.408
LIZ FONG-JONES: That is a
very complicated and fraught

00:10:20.408 --> 00:10:22.100
question at Google
because of the fact

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:25.370
that Google came up with
SRE at about the same time

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:29.270
that other people were coming up
with DevOps outside of Google.

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:31.710
I definitely would
say that when we're

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:35.390
talking about SRE techniques
and SRE best practices,

00:10:35.390 --> 00:10:38.737
that practitioners of DevOps
will find that a lot of them

00:10:38.737 --> 00:10:39.320
apply to them.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:42.359
And conversely, we learn a
lot from the DevOps movement.

00:10:42.359 --> 00:10:44.150
There are a lot of
things that we take back

00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:45.317
in from the DevOps movement.

00:10:45.317 --> 00:10:47.274
MELANIE WARRICK: So I
guess that you could also

00:10:47.274 --> 00:10:49.070
say there are multiple
ways of implementing

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:50.825
DevOps outside of just SRE.

00:10:50.825 --> 00:10:51.950
LIZ FONG-JONES: Yes and no.

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:53.690
I think SRE is the
only one that actually

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:55.530
has a brand associated with it.

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:56.850
So I'll give you an example.

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:59.250
We take one of those pillars,
like accepting accidents

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:00.560
and failures as normal.

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:03.980
One of the things that many
people who implement DevOps do

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:08.226
is called postmortems, which is
after an outage or an incident,

00:11:08.226 --> 00:11:09.350
we collect all of the data.

00:11:09.350 --> 00:11:10.410
And we have a meeting--

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:11.284
it might be internal.

00:11:11.284 --> 00:11:12.470
It might be external--

00:11:12.470 --> 00:11:13.796
where we analyze everything.

00:11:13.796 --> 00:11:15.170
And we have next
steps to, how do

00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:16.320
we prevent this in the future?

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:18.820
Or if it happens in the future,
how do we reduce the impact?

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:20.960
In the SRE program,
that's a key pillar.

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:22.400
It's called out.

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:25.010
And in many other
organizations' DevOps programs,

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:26.720
it's a key pillar
that's called out.

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:29.840
But actually in no part of the
DevOps manifesto does it say,

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:31.280
you must practice postmortems.

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:34.190
So I think there are many
ways to implement DevOps.

00:11:34.190 --> 00:11:35.930
I think SRE is the
only one that I'm

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:38.840
aware of that actually kind of
has a name and its own brand

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:40.140
associated with it.

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:41.598
MELANIE WARRICK:
What kind of tools

00:11:41.598 --> 00:11:44.370
are your favorite tools to
use in your respective spaces

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:45.704
of DevOps and SRE?

00:11:45.704 --> 00:11:47.870
SETH VARGO: You know, DevOps
is a cultural movement.

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:49.520
It's not a technology movement.

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:51.950
So everyone kind of
has their own things

00:11:51.950 --> 00:11:53.120
that they like to use.

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:56.060
For me, I like tools that
practice infrastructure as code

00:11:56.060 --> 00:11:57.690
and increase collaboration.

00:11:57.690 --> 00:12:01.620
So tools like Terraform,
tools like Chef and Puppet

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:04.700
and Ansible and Salt that let
us take these abstract concepts

00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:06.530
like infrastructure--
cloud providers

00:12:06.530 --> 00:12:09.350
like Google Cloud, systems
like a Debian system--

00:12:09.350 --> 00:12:12.770
and capture the
code and the process

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:14.377
in a not only
repeatable manner--

00:12:14.377 --> 00:12:15.710
so we can talk about automation.

00:12:15.710 --> 00:12:18.380
But what I like is that we
can focus on collaboration.

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:19.920
We capture this as code.

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:22.130
And now we can put it in a
pull request and a source

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:22.910
repository.

00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:24.710
And we can collaborate
on the changes.

00:12:24.710 --> 00:12:26.210
We can have our
teammates coming in.

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:27.860
And it's one thing to
have someone standing

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:29.540
over your shoulder in a
terminal saying, yeah,

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:31.340
that command looks
good, and another thing

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:33.890
to be able to actually
comment on a pull request

00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:36.510
on something like GitHub and
say, yeah, this change is good.

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:39.260
So I tend to like tools that
obviously solve a technology

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:41.510
problem, but they do it in
a way that is collaborative

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:43.010
and encourages collaboration.

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:45.769
So I think that's one of the key
pillars of the DevOps movement.

00:12:45.769 --> 00:12:47.060
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice, and Liz?

00:12:47.060 --> 00:12:49.250
LIZ FONG-JONES: I think
that the key to SRE

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:52.680
is not necessarily using
one specific set of tools,

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:55.370
but instead saying, you
know, you have to use a tool.

00:12:55.370 --> 00:12:59.300
Pick one to accomplish this
particular set of objectives.

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:01.010
For instance, I agree
with Seth that you

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:03.260
have to have some
kind of mechanism

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:06.080
of having your config as
code-- having it checked in,

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:08.120
having it repeatable,
having it reproducible,

00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:10.152
having it be usable
by automation.

00:13:10.152 --> 00:13:12.110
And I don't care which
particular tool you use.

00:13:12.110 --> 00:13:14.401
And in fact, most of the
tools that I used in my career

00:13:14.401 --> 00:13:16.980
at Google have been tools that
are proprietary to Google.

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:17.840
And that's fine.

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:19.889
You know, there are plenty
of equally good tools

00:13:19.889 --> 00:13:21.680
available outside of
Google that accomplish

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:23.870
the same goals for people
that don't work at Google.

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:25.828
Another thing that I'd
recommend, for instance,

00:13:25.828 --> 00:13:29.300
is you have to use something
to monitor your systems.

00:13:29.300 --> 00:13:30.980
I don't care whether
it's Prometheus,

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:33.188
or whether it's Datadog, or
whether it's Stackdriver.

00:13:33.188 --> 00:13:35.480
I just care that you pick
a tool and stick to it.

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:37.360
It's better than muddling
around in the dark.

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:39.568
SETH VARGO: Yeah, and I
think to double down on that,

00:13:39.568 --> 00:13:42.240
it's not just important
that you pick one.

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:45.180
In a large organization, it's
important that there's buy in.

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:46.890
You can't have the
team over there

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:49.356
using Datadog and the team
over there using Prometheus.

00:13:49.356 --> 00:13:50.730
That might work
at a small scale.

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:52.830
But these systems all need
to talk to each other.

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:54.720
So you need a centralized
source for things

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:56.070
like monitoring and
alerting, or else it's

00:13:56.070 --> 00:13:58.090
going to be very difficult
to actually visualize

00:13:58.090 --> 00:14:00.048
how these systems are
operating and functioning

00:14:00.048 --> 00:14:01.030
at a very high level.

00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:02.580
MELANIE WARRICK: Which plays
into your whole fundamental

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:04.600
around collaboration
to begin with, right?

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:05.840
SETH VARGO: Right.

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:08.940
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, and it sounds
like also the culture of SRE

00:14:08.940 --> 00:14:10.740
and DevOps within
your organization

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:12.520
needs to be-- like,
it's a culture.

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:13.710
It's a company culture.

00:14:13.710 --> 00:14:14.380
SETH VARGO: Yes, definitely.

00:14:14.380 --> 00:14:15.755
LIZ FONG-JONES:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:14:15.755 --> 00:14:18.090
And I think that ties into
another element of needing

00:14:18.090 --> 00:14:19.740
institutional support, right?

00:14:19.740 --> 00:14:23.910
Someone needs to provide a
career path for your SREs

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:25.800
or for your people
practicing DevOps.

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:28.960
You can't really say, you know,
this is my one DevOps engineer.

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:29.927
This is my one SRE.

00:14:29.927 --> 00:14:32.010
And they're just going to
do magical stuff, right?

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:33.780
You have to have a
community of practice.

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:35.790
You have to have a mechanism
of supporting them.

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:36.460
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:14:36.460 --> 00:14:37.470
Liz, you said something earlier.

00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:39.303
And you said you were
going to allude to it.

00:14:39.303 --> 00:14:40.540
So I'll pull it up now.

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:44.290
You were talking about
sort of SLIs, SLOs, SLAs.

00:14:44.290 --> 00:14:46.320
We've talked about them
previously a little bit

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:47.040
on the podcast.

00:14:47.040 --> 00:14:50.790
But people who haven't
listened to previous episodes--

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:51.970
what are these things?

00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:53.230
Why do we care about them?

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:53.477
LIZ FONG-JONES: Sure.

00:14:53.477 --> 00:14:55.080
So I'm happy to break that down.

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:57.990
So we're talking about
three key terms--

00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:00.200
a Service Level
Indicator, or SLI,

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:03.150
a Service Level Objective,
or SLO, and a Service Level

00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:04.950
Agreement, or SLA.

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:07.860
And a service level indicator
is the basic building block

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:08.460
of that.

00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:11.940
It's a mechanism of deciding, is
your service available enough?

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:14.850
Is it healthy enough that
in this instantaneous moment

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:16.170
you would consider it good?

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:18.120
And then over time,
you can aggregate that

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:21.300
into a overall service
level objective that says,

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:23.940
x percent of the time, my
service level indicators

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:25.720
should be satisfied.

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:28.170
So you might decide
it's OK for my service

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:31.680
to be down for 10 minutes
out of every month.

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:34.170
Or it's OK for my service
to be down 20 minutes

00:15:34.170 --> 00:15:36.670
out of every month, or two
hours out of every month.

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:38.669
So you have to decide on
what that threshold is.

00:15:38.669 --> 00:15:41.334
And that percentage is a
service level objective.

00:15:41.334 --> 00:15:42.750
And then a service
level agreement

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:45.760
is a promise that you make to
someone that has consequences.

00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:49.350
For instance, you might
agree to remediate the issue

00:15:49.350 --> 00:15:50.550
and provide a post-mortem.

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:53.550
Or you might agree to provide
money back to a customer

00:15:53.550 --> 00:15:55.592
if you fail to meet your
service level agreement.

00:15:55.592 --> 00:15:57.466
So to break down things
a little bit further,

00:15:57.466 --> 00:15:59.100
a service level
indicator specifically

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:02.490
tends to be some kind of
key performance metric.

00:16:02.490 --> 00:16:05.776
And then it's subject to some
kind of a windowing function

00:16:05.776 --> 00:16:06.900
and subject to a threshold.

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:09.360
For instance, I might
say my key metric

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:13.350
is how fast does it take to load
the home page of Google.com?

00:16:13.350 --> 00:16:17.220
And then I might say, OK,
I want the 95th percentile

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:19.600
of that latency distribution.

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:22.140
And I want to make sure
that that's always less

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:24.510
than, say, 150 milliseconds.

00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:26.850
So then that generates,
at any moment in time,

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:27.810
a yes or no value.

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:29.670
Is it good enough or not?

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:33.930
And then I might say, I aim to
have that satisfied, let's say,

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:36.600
99.99% of the time.

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:38.910
And then that gives
me an idea as to

00:16:38.910 --> 00:16:40.200
is my service healthy enough?

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:42.760
Do I have room in
order to experiment?

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:45.540
Do I have room in order
to do deployments?

00:16:45.540 --> 00:16:49.080
Do I have room in order to
do more experimental things?

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:50.789
Or do I need to slow down?

00:16:50.789 --> 00:16:52.330
Do I need to say,
hey, wait a second.

00:16:52.330 --> 00:16:54.538
We need to focus on stability
for the next few months

00:16:54.538 --> 00:16:57.091
rather than on deploying as
many new features as possible?

00:16:57.091 --> 00:16:59.340
So that's kind of leading
into the concept of an error

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:03.000
budget of the difference between
one and your service level

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:03.635
objective.

00:17:03.635 --> 00:17:05.010
And then how much
of that is left

00:17:05.010 --> 00:17:07.290
to spend based on how
available you've been

00:17:07.290 --> 00:17:09.760
over the past number of months?

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:11.910
SETH VARGO: So Liz, all
that makes a lot of sense.

00:17:11.910 --> 00:17:14.832
But how do you deal with a
situation where you have--

00:17:14.832 --> 00:17:16.290
we haven't mentioned
it yet, but we

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:18.706
know that they're called error
budgets, which is basically

00:17:18.706 --> 00:17:22.260
once that SLO is exhausted,
the remaining time

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:23.730
is an error budget.

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:27.359
How do you just prevent those
teams from just ignoring it?

00:17:27.359 --> 00:17:30.750
How do the SREs have power
within the organization

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:33.090
to push back on the developers
so that they're not just

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:35.760
ignoring these
agreed-upon criteria?

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:38.615
LIZ FONG-JONES: It's a manner of
collaboration that the service

00:17:38.615 --> 00:17:40.410
level objective arises--

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:42.480
the target itself comes
from a collaboration

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:44.940
between product management SRE
and the product development

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:47.200
software engineering
organization.

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:49.500
And we are empowered
to push back.

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:53.434
And we are empowered to
escalate, if needed, to say,

00:17:53.434 --> 00:17:55.350
we're endangering the
service level objective.

00:17:55.350 --> 00:17:56.580
We need to cut back.

00:17:56.580 --> 00:17:59.520
Or we need to do
engineering changes,

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:03.370
and to get people
to agree on that.

00:18:03.370 --> 00:18:05.850
And in the end,
if someone insists

00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:09.600
on doing super risky things
and breaking the error budget,

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:13.560
then we have the right to pull
SREs off of a project and say,

00:18:13.560 --> 00:18:14.619
you know what?

00:18:14.619 --> 00:18:16.410
You can go and hire
more software engineers

00:18:16.410 --> 00:18:18.750
to replace the SREs
that are stepping away.

00:18:18.750 --> 00:18:21.060
But SREs are scarce
and valuable.

00:18:21.060 --> 00:18:23.580
And we think that there are
more projects waiting in line

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:26.730
to have SRE support that are
willing to stick to their error

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:27.930
budgets and SLOs.

00:18:27.930 --> 00:18:28.500
MELANIE WARRICK:
Wow, that's great

00:18:28.500 --> 00:18:30.270
that you guys have that
type of empowerment.

00:18:30.270 --> 00:18:31.811
LIZ FONG-JONES: We
know that it's not

00:18:31.811 --> 00:18:34.950
universal across all
sizes of organizations.

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:37.200
I think the key thing that
anyone listening to this

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:39.009
should take away is
the idea that you

00:18:39.009 --> 00:18:40.800
need to have some kind
of executive sponsor

00:18:40.800 --> 00:18:43.260
that's willing to
decide, here's where

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:44.800
I'm going to allocate SREs.

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:46.734
Here's what's acceptable
for them or not.

00:18:46.734 --> 00:18:48.150
And if you don't
have that person,

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:49.900
your SREs are probably
going to eventually leave

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:51.730
if the error budgets
keep on getting broken,

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:53.646
and they keep getting
paged and feel that they

00:18:53.646 --> 00:18:54.780
can't do anything about it.

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:57.071
MELANIE WARRICK: Are there
anything else that you'd say

00:18:57.071 --> 00:19:00.325
is crucial to make SRE
a success for companies?

00:19:00.325 --> 00:19:01.950
LIZ FONG-JONES: I
think the other thing

00:19:01.950 --> 00:19:05.400
that's essential to making
SREs successful, besides having

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:08.270
executive sponsorship and
having agreed upon service level

00:19:08.270 --> 00:19:12.180
objectives, is the idea of
measuring how much time you're

00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:14.820
spending doing
operational work, or toil,

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:17.520
as we call it-- the idea
that we should measure

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:20.325
how much of our time do
we spend that's doing work

00:19:20.325 --> 00:19:22.350
to stay running in place?

00:19:22.350 --> 00:19:24.000
Stuff like doing
manual deployments.

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:26.180
Stuff like responding
to tickets.

00:19:26.180 --> 00:19:29.579
Stuff like making sure that the
system is fixed once it breaks.

00:19:29.579 --> 00:19:31.620
Versus what are we doing
to make the service more

00:19:31.620 --> 00:19:33.600
sustainable and scalable
in the long term?

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:35.229
What are our long-term projects?

00:19:35.229 --> 00:19:37.770
So that idea that we should have
a budget of how much time we

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:41.490
spend on toil and keep that
below half the time that we

00:19:41.490 --> 00:19:44.850
have in the day, I think that's
kind of a key practice of SRE

00:19:44.850 --> 00:19:49.560
that prevents it from degrading
into having a operations

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:51.750
team running around
not really empowered

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:53.100
to do engineering work.

00:19:53.100 --> 00:19:54.840
That's the thing
that we use to guide,

00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:56.460
is are we spending
less than half

00:19:56.460 --> 00:19:58.280
of our time doing that
manual work or not?

00:19:58.280 --> 00:19:59.655
MELANIE WARRICK:
Seth, I know you

00:19:59.655 --> 00:20:02.070
were talking before about
how much collaboration

00:20:02.070 --> 00:20:04.290
is a priority from a
DevOps perspective.

00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:07.800
And I assume you've got
similar, like-- executive buy-in

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:09.740
as being something that's
a factor of success.

00:20:09.740 --> 00:20:12.720
Are there any other factors
of successes that you think

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:14.595
are very crucial for
DevOps to be successful?

00:20:14.595 --> 00:20:15.969
SETH VARGO: Yeah,
I mean, I think

00:20:15.969 --> 00:20:17.670
automation, which
we kind of already

00:20:17.670 --> 00:20:20.500
touched on a little bit, is one
of the key factors of DevOps.

00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:23.460
It's this idea that
people get burned out

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:26.812
as they continuously don't
bring value to the organization.

00:20:26.812 --> 00:20:28.770
And this is traditionally
one of the challenges

00:20:28.770 --> 00:20:31.290
with operations in general
is that if you're not

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:33.010
bringing value to
the organization,

00:20:33.010 --> 00:20:34.560
it's really hard to
retain employees.

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:36.893
Because they don't feel like
the work that they're doing

00:20:36.893 --> 00:20:38.630
benefits the company.

00:20:38.630 --> 00:20:41.144
And SRE, it's Site
Reliability Engineering.

00:20:41.144 --> 00:20:42.810
They're engineers at
the end of the day.

00:20:42.810 --> 00:20:46.290
And DevOps-- typically
folks practicing DevOps

00:20:46.290 --> 00:20:49.050
have engineering backgrounds,
like in software or computer

00:20:49.050 --> 00:20:49.772
science.

00:20:49.772 --> 00:20:51.730
So they want to actually
be engineering, right?

00:20:51.730 --> 00:20:53.313
And they might not
want to be building

00:20:53.313 --> 00:20:54.630
self-balancing red black trees.

00:20:54.630 --> 00:20:55.710
But at the same
time, they don't want

00:20:55.710 --> 00:20:57.630
to be running the
tail command all day.

00:20:57.630 --> 00:21:00.810
So finding that balance
between the toil

00:21:00.810 --> 00:21:02.730
and touching the
system manually,

00:21:02.730 --> 00:21:06.030
versus building these really
great systems, whether it's

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:09.600
a custom homegrown CI/CD
for delivering things,

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:12.090
or custom alerting and
monitoring solution,

00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:15.930
or just integrating
automation and orchestration

00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:20.400
across the entire cluster
to be able to bring value

00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:21.780
to the daily lives.

00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:23.400
MARK MANDEL: Actually,
to that point,

00:21:23.400 --> 00:21:25.050
you're talking a lot
about automation.

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:28.070
Are you factoring in
stuff like SLOs or SLAs

00:21:28.070 --> 00:21:30.180
or something similar in
the DevOps space that

00:21:30.180 --> 00:21:31.620
would translate through to SRE?

00:21:31.620 --> 00:21:33.455
Or how does those two things--

00:21:33.455 --> 00:21:34.590
do they work together?

00:21:34.590 --> 00:21:36.870
SETH VARGO: So there's
nothing specifically in kind

00:21:36.870 --> 00:21:39.600
of the DevOps manifesto
that says how to measure

00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:41.407
things, other than measure.

00:21:41.407 --> 00:21:42.990
So this is one of
the big differences,

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:45.770
and kind of why we say, like,
class SRE implements DevOps,

00:21:45.770 --> 00:21:47.862
is the DevOps says you
should measure stuff.

00:21:47.862 --> 00:21:49.320
And it doesn't tell
you whether you

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:51.480
should be using the metric
system or the English system.

00:21:51.480 --> 00:21:53.563
It doesn't tell you you
should be using Prometheus

00:21:53.563 --> 00:21:54.840
or Stackdriver or Datadog.

00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:56.610
So it's up to
individual organizations

00:21:56.610 --> 00:21:59.100
to make that decision,
whereas the SRE discipline

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:01.530
is very much along the
lines of leveraging tools

00:22:01.530 --> 00:22:03.510
like Stackdriver, et cetera.

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:06.210
LIZ FONG-JONES: And I think that
that's an area where we've seen

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:08.560
a little bit of divergence
between DevOps and SRE that

00:22:08.560 --> 00:22:10.050
we're hoping to kind of mend--

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:11.930
this idea of, do
we measure how many

00:22:11.930 --> 00:22:13.560
deploys per day that we do?

00:22:13.560 --> 00:22:16.510
What is the metric that we use
to measure customer happiness?

00:22:16.510 --> 00:22:18.810
And that's why I think that
it's important for everyone

00:22:18.810 --> 00:22:21.260
to start adopting
service level objectives,

00:22:21.260 --> 00:22:24.030
because a good service
objective is focused on,

00:22:24.030 --> 00:22:26.070
what does the customer
actually want to achieve?

00:22:26.070 --> 00:22:27.960
Can they achieve what they want?

00:22:27.960 --> 00:22:32.340
Rather than kind of focusing
on, how many percent

00:22:32.340 --> 00:22:34.350
Pingdom success do I have?

00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:37.433
Or, am I shipping 1,000
deploys a day, but all of them

00:22:37.433 --> 00:22:39.016
are breaking the
build, or all of them

00:22:39.016 --> 00:22:40.950
are making customers unhappy?

00:22:40.950 --> 00:22:43.380
We have to agree
across our industry

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:45.100
on what makes customers happy.

00:22:45.100 --> 00:22:46.770
Are we converging
on the right number?

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:49.842
And is it something that
is sustainable for us?

00:22:49.842 --> 00:22:51.550
SETH VARGO: Yeah, and
I think to rephrase

00:22:51.550 --> 00:22:54.050
that in a slightly different
way is that the DevOps movement

00:22:54.050 --> 00:22:58.180
evolved out of making developers
and operators happier,

00:22:58.180 --> 00:23:00.360
whereas the SRE
movement evolved out

00:23:00.360 --> 00:23:03.690
of increasing customer
happiness by building

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:04.680
more reliable systems.

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:05.805
And what you find is that--

00:23:05.805 --> 00:23:08.179
Liz, I don't know if you agree
or disagree with me here--

00:23:08.179 --> 00:23:10.410
but the practices are
actually pretty similar.

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:12.618
But that's why you'll tend
to see the DevOps movement

00:23:12.618 --> 00:23:14.640
focus on things like
deploys per day,

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:16.800
whereas the SRE teams
will focus more on,

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:19.860
like, latency and
ping time and render

00:23:19.860 --> 00:23:22.657
time of the actual interactions
of customers or users.

00:23:22.657 --> 00:23:23.740
MARK MANDEL: Do you agree?

00:23:23.740 --> 00:23:25.230
LIZ FONG-JONES:
Yeah, I totally do.

00:23:25.230 --> 00:23:27.530
MELANIE WARRICK: Seth, you
briefly talked about CI/CD.

00:23:27.530 --> 00:23:30.671
Is CI/CD required
to do DevOps in SRE?

00:23:30.671 --> 00:23:31.920
SETH VARGO: Again, yes and no.

00:23:31.920 --> 00:23:34.110
It's not specifically part
of the DevOps manifesto.

00:23:34.110 --> 00:23:36.810
But automation is
a key pillar here.

00:23:36.810 --> 00:23:39.360
And having these continuous
integration continuous delivery

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:42.210
services such that I can push
some code-- whether that code

00:23:42.210 --> 00:23:44.490
is to provision infrastructure
or an application--

00:23:44.490 --> 00:23:46.710
and I can test it in
an automated fashion.

00:23:46.710 --> 00:23:48.960
While that's happening, I
can be doing other work.

00:23:48.960 --> 00:23:51.690
I can bring other value to the
system or the organization.

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:53.850
And at the same time,
what you'll find

00:23:53.850 --> 00:23:56.370
is that CI/CD is closely
related to collaboration.

00:23:56.370 --> 00:23:58.854
Having the ability to perform
continuous integration

00:23:58.854 --> 00:24:00.270
and continuous
delivery ultimately

00:24:00.270 --> 00:24:01.519
leads to higher collaboration.

00:24:01.519 --> 00:24:03.480
So that's why it's part
of the DevOps movement,

00:24:03.480 --> 00:24:06.719
but it's not specifically
required to do DevOps.

00:24:06.719 --> 00:24:08.760
MARK MANDEL: Actually,
now that I think about it,

00:24:08.760 --> 00:24:10.860
in previous times we've
talked about SRE culture,

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:13.080
we haven't really talked
about continuous integration

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:13.680
and development.

00:24:13.680 --> 00:24:16.180
But that seems like something
that would potentially reduce,

00:24:16.180 --> 00:24:18.600
like, basically errors
and things going wrong.

00:24:18.600 --> 00:24:19.860
Liz, where does that fit in?

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:21.660
LIZ FONG-JONES:
So similarly, SRE

00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:24.690
doesn't say you
have to do CI/CD.

00:24:24.690 --> 00:24:28.020
But we do rigorous risk
analysis to figure out

00:24:28.020 --> 00:24:30.750
what risk factors might prevent
us from meeting our service

00:24:30.750 --> 00:24:32.280
level objectives.

00:24:32.280 --> 00:24:36.420
And it winds up being the
case that if you are manually

00:24:36.420 --> 00:24:39.180
doing deploys, and if you're
manually doing builds,

00:24:39.180 --> 00:24:41.730
you're going to be delivering
defective software more often.

00:24:41.730 --> 00:24:44.790
You're going to be spending
a lot of time rolling back

00:24:44.790 --> 00:24:46.210
in case of outages.

00:24:46.210 --> 00:24:48.210
And that adds up and eats
into your error budget

00:24:48.210 --> 00:24:50.890
and eventually leads
to unhappy customers.

00:24:50.890 --> 00:24:53.370
So we practice CI and
CD as a consequence

00:24:53.370 --> 00:24:55.560
of wanting to fulfill
our error budgets,

00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:58.440
and as a consequence of wanting
to keep the amount of manual

00:24:58.440 --> 00:25:00.560
toil that we do under control.

00:25:00.560 --> 00:25:04.470
In SRE, we do error
budgets and toil budgets.

00:25:04.470 --> 00:25:07.080
And we also have baked
into our systems,

00:25:07.080 --> 00:25:09.060
because of a lot of our
developer productivity

00:25:09.060 --> 00:25:13.277
tooling, the notion of having
reproducible builds already

00:25:13.277 --> 00:25:15.360
done for us, that the
product development software

00:25:15.360 --> 00:25:17.970
engineers already come
to us with these things.

00:25:17.970 --> 00:25:19.936
And they typically
need help with,

00:25:19.936 --> 00:25:21.810
how do we roll this out
in a scalable matter?

00:25:21.810 --> 00:25:23.850
How do we detect
failures earlier?

00:25:23.850 --> 00:25:26.640
So it's kind of working on more
of the advanced aspects of CD

00:25:26.640 --> 00:25:27.700
where we really shine.

00:25:27.700 --> 00:25:28.680
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:25:28.680 --> 00:25:30.570
On a slightly
different note, how

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:32.460
do you collaborate
with security teams?

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:36.150
Or what are best practices
for SRE or DevOps

00:25:36.150 --> 00:25:39.154
to integrate into and
communicate with security?

00:25:39.154 --> 00:25:41.070
SETH VARGO: There's
actually this sub-movement

00:25:41.070 --> 00:25:44.280
of DevOps called DevSecOps,
the Sec obviously standing

00:25:44.280 --> 00:25:45.950
for Security there
in the middle.

00:25:45.950 --> 00:25:48.491
There's also Dev Marketing
Ops and-- you can put any word

00:25:48.491 --> 00:25:49.740
in the middle that you'd like.

00:25:49.740 --> 00:25:53.220
And it's this idea that
security, just like operations,

00:25:53.220 --> 00:25:56.370
shouldn't be its own department
that does its own thing.

00:25:56.370 --> 00:25:58.680
Instead, it should be
embedded with these teams who

00:25:58.680 --> 00:26:00.150
are working closely.

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:02.190
It's much easier to
fix a software problem,

00:26:02.190 --> 00:26:04.410
whether it's security
or operations,

00:26:04.410 --> 00:26:06.360
closer to the
codebase than once it

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:08.190
reaches this arbitrary
governing body that

00:26:08.190 --> 00:26:09.720
says, yes, this is secure.

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:10.541
No, this is not.

00:26:10.541 --> 00:26:12.540
And what you'll also find
is that having someone

00:26:12.540 --> 00:26:14.610
embedded on the team who
is security-minded or

00:26:14.610 --> 00:26:17.340
security-focused can
often have more context

00:26:17.340 --> 00:26:19.950
going into a security
review than just handling

00:26:19.950 --> 00:26:22.510
thousands of lines of code
over to some third party team.

00:26:22.510 --> 00:26:23.970
And not only does
it take them time

00:26:23.970 --> 00:26:26.053
to understand the code and
the goal of the system,

00:26:26.053 --> 00:26:28.200
but then also they have
to review it for security.

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:30.670
Obviously security is
something paramount to Google.

00:26:30.670 --> 00:26:32.770
It's something that
runs in our blood.

00:26:32.770 --> 00:26:35.030
So we have dedicated
security teams here.

00:26:35.030 --> 00:26:36.780
But we also have people
who are constantly

00:26:36.780 --> 00:26:38.905
thinking about security
from a software engineering

00:26:38.905 --> 00:26:41.910
perspective embedded
on different teams.

00:26:41.910 --> 00:26:44.160
And the DevSecOps
kind of culture

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:45.475
is really the same thing.

00:26:45.475 --> 00:26:47.850
Let's not just break down the
barriers between developers

00:26:47.850 --> 00:26:48.580
and operators.

00:26:48.580 --> 00:26:51.570
Let's break down the barriers
between developers, operators,

00:26:51.570 --> 00:26:53.340
and security teams,
because they're also

00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:56.190
traditionally viewed as
kind of this place where

00:26:56.190 --> 00:26:58.959
dreams go to die, for
lack of a better word.

00:26:58.959 --> 00:27:00.750
There's two places
where dreams go to die--

00:27:00.750 --> 00:27:00.840
MELANIE WARRICK: I like this.

00:27:00.840 --> 00:27:03.006
MARK MANDEL: --the security
team and the legal team.

00:27:03.006 --> 00:27:05.805
And if we can get those
teams to collaborate and work

00:27:05.805 --> 00:27:07.680
closely from the beginning,
with the software

00:27:07.680 --> 00:27:10.500
engineers and the operators,
it improves the likelihood

00:27:10.500 --> 00:27:12.167
of the success of the
project over time.

00:27:12.167 --> 00:27:13.750
MELANIE WARRICK: And
Liz, did you want

00:27:13.750 --> 00:27:15.000
to add anything around SRE?

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:18.720
LIZ FONG-JONES: SRE and
security really go hand-in-hand.

00:27:18.720 --> 00:27:23.010
In particular, SREs are
empowered to shut off a service

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:25.560
if it poses a grave
security risk to Google.

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:26.520
Like, that's kind of--

00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:27.660
SETH VARGO: Big red button?

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:29.970
LIZ FONG-JONES: Yeah, you
have to put the users first.

00:27:29.970 --> 00:27:33.240
And protecting our users is the
first priority that we have,

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:35.760
even above the number of nines.

00:27:35.760 --> 00:27:38.130
It's better for Google to
be down than for Google

00:27:38.130 --> 00:27:41.310
to be posing a security
risk to our users.

00:27:41.310 --> 00:27:43.800
Furthermore, a lot of
SREs move back and forth

00:27:43.800 --> 00:27:45.847
between SRE and
security and privacy.

00:27:45.847 --> 00:27:47.430
The mindsets are
very closely aligned,

00:27:47.430 --> 00:27:50.310
because SREs are
constructively pessimists.

00:27:50.310 --> 00:27:52.740
And that's the case with
security and privacy teams,

00:27:52.740 --> 00:27:53.620
as well--

00:27:53.620 --> 00:27:55.867
that we wanted to help
you do the right thing.

00:27:55.867 --> 00:27:57.450
And that we're often
prepared to think

00:27:57.450 --> 00:27:58.740
of what can possibly go wrong?

00:27:58.740 --> 00:28:00.330
How can we guard against that?

00:28:00.330 --> 00:28:01.720
Rather than say, what
can possibly go wrong?

00:28:01.720 --> 00:28:03.011
And therefore you can't launch.

00:28:03.011 --> 00:28:04.410
No, that's not what we say.

00:28:04.410 --> 00:28:06.120
I think another
interesting nuance

00:28:06.120 --> 00:28:09.360
there is that the approach
of SRE teams at Google

00:28:09.360 --> 00:28:11.820
is closely aligned with
privacy and security,

00:28:11.820 --> 00:28:14.190
with regard to building
frameworks that do

00:28:14.190 --> 00:28:15.750
the right thing out of the box.

00:28:15.750 --> 00:28:18.870
That's a thing that we've
started doing in the past six

00:28:18.870 --> 00:28:22.110
or seven years at Google to
really focus the development

00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:24.282
efforts in SRE
upon not just what

00:28:24.282 --> 00:28:25.740
do we do in one
individual product,

00:28:25.740 --> 00:28:27.690
but how do we make sure
that everything bakes

00:28:27.690 --> 00:28:31.410
in the right default, whether it
be for reliability or security

00:28:31.410 --> 00:28:32.490
or privacy?

00:28:32.490 --> 00:28:32.990
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:28:32.990 --> 00:28:33.489
Thanks, Liz.

00:28:33.489 --> 00:28:35.550
So one other question
I have for you

00:28:35.550 --> 00:28:38.522
both is, what do you
enjoy most about SRE?

00:28:38.522 --> 00:28:40.980
LIZ FONG-JONES: I think the
thing I like the most about SRE

00:28:40.980 --> 00:28:43.230
is that it's really
a cross-disciplinary

00:28:43.230 --> 00:28:46.680
collaborative team, that
we have a whole variety

00:28:46.680 --> 00:28:49.180
of different SRE teams all up
and down the stack at Google

00:28:49.180 --> 00:28:51.390
that all work together every
single day to make sure

00:28:51.390 --> 00:28:53.700
that Google is
appropriately reliable.

00:28:53.700 --> 00:28:55.950
And also that we have
so many different skill

00:28:55.950 --> 00:28:57.600
sets on those teams,
whether people

00:28:57.600 --> 00:29:00.060
come from systems engineering
or systems administration

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:02.940
backgrounds, or traditional
software engineering computer

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:05.170
science backgrounds,
or humanities

00:29:05.170 --> 00:29:06.210
or physics backgrounds.

00:29:06.210 --> 00:29:08.530
Like, we all work
together as a team.

00:29:08.530 --> 00:29:10.470
And we solve these really
challenging problems

00:29:10.470 --> 00:29:12.540
and make Google
run at that scale.

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:14.820
And that's what really
makes me love my job.

00:29:14.820 --> 00:29:16.611
MELANIE WARRICK: And
then Seth, what do you

00:29:16.611 --> 00:29:17.655
enjoy most about DevOps?

00:29:17.655 --> 00:29:19.530
SETH VARGO: I mean,
basically the same thing.

00:29:19.530 --> 00:29:22.030
The DevOps community is, like,
this incredibly diverse group

00:29:22.030 --> 00:29:25.470
of people, ranging from
folks with backgrounds

00:29:25.470 --> 00:29:29.100
in theater and arts and
software and math and physics.

00:29:29.100 --> 00:29:31.480
And being able to not only
interact with those people,

00:29:31.480 --> 00:29:34.350
but see how their experiences
and their backgrounds

00:29:34.350 --> 00:29:36.270
bring different
solutions to mind--

00:29:36.270 --> 00:29:38.727
like, that collaborative
nature is truly inspiring

00:29:38.727 --> 00:29:40.560
to see the different
ways that people think,

00:29:40.560 --> 00:29:42.480
and the different ways they
arrive at conclusions--

00:29:42.480 --> 00:29:44.220
sometimes the same,
sometimes different--

00:29:44.220 --> 00:29:46.140
and how that can lead
to ultimately making

00:29:46.140 --> 00:29:48.332
better software and
making customers happier.

00:29:48.332 --> 00:29:50.790
MARK MANDEL: And if people want
to learn more about DevOps,

00:29:50.790 --> 00:29:53.647
do you have any particular
places you like to send people?

00:29:53.647 --> 00:29:55.230
SETH VARGO: Sure,
you can all call me.

00:29:55.230 --> 00:29:56.350
My phone number is--

00:29:56.350 --> 00:29:57.580
I'm just kidding.

00:29:57.580 --> 00:30:00.060
So there's quite a few
places to learn about DevOps.

00:30:00.060 --> 00:30:01.950
There's obviously, like,
Wikipedia and stuff.

00:30:01.950 --> 00:30:04.320
DevOps is a little bit weird,
because there is no one

00:30:04.320 --> 00:30:06.780
true definition of DevOps.

00:30:06.780 --> 00:30:08.520
It's something that
a couple companies

00:30:08.520 --> 00:30:10.860
have tried to grab onto and
claim it as their own brand.

00:30:10.860 --> 00:30:12.840
But ultimately, it's
an organic movement

00:30:12.840 --> 00:30:13.840
started by a community.

00:30:13.840 --> 00:30:16.860
So everyone does have kind of
their own slight definition

00:30:16.860 --> 00:30:19.890
or slight lens which
they see through DevOps.

00:30:19.890 --> 00:30:21.450
So I actually
recommend people look

00:30:21.450 --> 00:30:23.427
at what DevOps is
not before they start

00:30:23.427 --> 00:30:24.510
looking at what DevOps is.

00:30:24.510 --> 00:30:26.355
And there's a lot of
really good articles

00:30:26.355 --> 00:30:28.230
out on the internet that
will explicitly say,

00:30:28.230 --> 00:30:30.240
like, this is not DevOps.

00:30:30.240 --> 00:30:33.000
A quick search of the
most popular search engine

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:34.900
will show you, what is DevOps?

00:30:34.900 --> 00:30:38.285
Look for things like myths of
DevOps, or what is not DevOps?

00:30:38.285 --> 00:30:40.410
And you'll start to get a
better idea of what it is

00:30:40.410 --> 00:30:41.310
and how you can start.

00:30:41.310 --> 00:30:41.700
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:30:41.700 --> 00:30:43.076
We'll put some in
the show notes.

00:30:43.076 --> 00:30:44.908
Liz, if people want to
get involved with SRE

00:30:44.908 --> 00:30:47.370
or learn more about SRE, where
do you think they should go?

00:30:47.370 --> 00:30:50.070
