00:32:33.630 --> 00:32:35.546
And I need some computer
resources to do that.

00:32:35.546 --> 00:32:37.590
Is there a way where
Google Cloud can give me

00:32:37.590 --> 00:32:39.424
some possible
credits or anything

00:32:39.424 --> 00:32:41.340
like that to make my
life a little bit easier?

00:32:41.340 --> 00:32:42.306
[TINKLING CHIMES]

00:32:42.306 --> 00:32:43.639
MELANIE WARRICK: Why, yes, Mark.

00:32:43.639 --> 00:32:45.540
There is.

00:32:45.540 --> 00:32:46.649
I'm glad you asked.

00:32:46.649 --> 00:32:48.690
And the reason why there
is is because we've just

00:32:48.690 --> 00:32:51.510
announced a new credits program
for researchers specifically.

00:32:51.510 --> 00:32:53.070
Which, for our
listeners out there

00:32:53.070 --> 00:32:55.090
who might have heard us
talk about this in the past,

00:32:55.090 --> 00:32:56.464
there's been a
couple of programs

00:32:56.464 --> 00:32:59.620
we've talked about for
education and for other areas.

00:32:59.620 --> 00:33:01.410
But this is
specifically targeted

00:33:01.410 --> 00:33:04.620
for researchers, and researchers
who, as Mark was saying,

00:33:04.620 --> 00:33:07.170
are from accredited
academic institutions

00:33:07.170 --> 00:33:08.107
for certain countries.

00:33:08.107 --> 00:33:09.940
It's limited right now
to certain countries.

00:33:09.940 --> 00:33:11.910
And there's a list on the site.

00:33:11.910 --> 00:33:15.252
The amount of the
award is $5,000 US.

00:33:15.252 --> 00:33:16.710
And there's an
application process.

00:33:16.710 --> 00:33:17.792
And it's an ongoing--

00:33:17.792 --> 00:33:19.250
it's a rolling
application process.

00:33:19.250 --> 00:33:21.540
So it's not like a
specific time frame.

00:33:21.540 --> 00:33:23.449
I know that I regularly
get this question

00:33:23.449 --> 00:33:25.740
from people I talk to who
are out there in the research

00:33:25.740 --> 00:33:26.550
communities.

00:33:26.550 --> 00:33:28.740
You know, the biggest need
they have right now is

00:33:28.740 --> 00:33:29.520
compute resources.

00:33:29.520 --> 00:33:31.860
So this program is
specifically targeted for them

00:33:31.860 --> 00:33:33.480
and to help expand.

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:36.090
And it's interesting, too,
because on the link that we'll

00:33:36.090 --> 00:33:38.100
share in the show notes,
they talk about some

00:33:38.100 --> 00:33:40.016
of the different programs
that it's supporting

00:33:40.016 --> 00:33:42.810
and just a significant
amount of petabytes of data.

00:33:42.810 --> 00:33:44.520
They're using it at--

00:33:44.520 --> 00:33:47.640
Ryan Abernathy, who's
Assistant Professor of Earth

00:33:47.640 --> 00:33:48.930
and Environmental Sciences.

00:33:48.930 --> 00:33:52.650
The Ocean and Climate Physics
at the Lamont-Doherty Earth

00:33:52.650 --> 00:33:54.450
Observatory at
Columbia University

00:33:54.450 --> 00:33:56.666
is using this through
an NSF partnership.

00:33:56.666 --> 00:33:59.040
And with his team they're
developing open source platform

00:33:59.040 --> 00:34:01.200
to manage the complex data
sets of climate science.

00:34:01.200 --> 00:34:01.950
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:34:01.950 --> 00:34:03.366
MELANIE WARRICK:
And this platform

00:34:03.366 --> 00:34:06.530
called Pangeo can run Earth
systems modeling simulations

00:34:06.530 --> 00:34:09.120
on petabytes of high-resolution
three-dimensional data.

00:34:09.120 --> 00:34:09.650
MARK MANDEL: That's cool.

00:34:09.650 --> 00:34:10.983
MELANIE WARRICK: It's very cool.

00:34:10.983 --> 00:34:12.860
It's cool to solve
science problems.

